        "note": "Wright draws the bar as an ISLAND because the 1833-34 cut had made it one. In 1812 there was no cut, the spit ran to the mainland, and the river was deflected south behind it - which is the whole reason the mouth stood where Swearingen found it. So the attachment is a deduction from the landform's own behaviour and is graded inferred. The STRAIGHT LINE between the two points is a convenience and not a reading: nothing says the isthmus ran straight, how wide it was, or where its lake face lay. Drawing it is T-1243's problem, in the terrain spec, where an elevation would have to be argued for. See docs/LIBERTIES.md L240.",
